voicemod-linux
===============

This project is an unofficial compatibility tool. It is not affiliated with,
endorsed by, or sponsored by Voicemod S.L.

What this repository contains
-----------------------------
This repository adapts code from
[brunos3d/voicemod-linux](https://github.com/brunos3d/voicemod-linux), which is
licensed under the MIT License. Its copyright and permission notice are
retained in [LICENSE](LICENSE). Changes in this repository are also MIT
licensed:

  - bin/voicemod-linux        launcher script
  - bin/voicemod-open-url     browser opener for login links
  - bridge/vadshim/           C source for a user-mode shim (avrt.dll)
  - bridge/registry/          Windows registry fragment
  - install.sh, uninstall.sh  one-command setup scripts
  - scripts/, share/, docs/   release packaging, desktop template, docs

The repository contains no compiled binaries. Release bundles additionally
contain the shim (avrt.dll) compiled from bridge/vadshim/vadshim.c; that binary
is also our own work under the MIT License, and the build is reproducible.

What this repository does NOT contain
-------------------------------------
No Voicemod code, binaries, assets, trademarks, or logos are distributed here.
The latest Voicemod application is downloaded at install time from Voicemod's
own servers (api.voicemod.net and its CDN) into the user's home directory. A
user may instead provide their own installer locally; it is not uploaded or
redistributed. You must have your own Voicemod account to use it. This tool
does not bypass payment, licensing, authentication, or any copy-protection
measure.

Purpose and legal basis
------------------------
The shim and registry fragment exist only to achieve interoperability between
the unmodified Voicemod application and a Linux audio stack (PipeWire) and
graphics stack, replacing the Windows-only kernel driver and system calls that
Wine does not provide. The application binary on disk is never modified.

In the European Union, Directive 2009/24/EC permits the acts needed to achieve
interoperability of an independently created program (Article 6), and declares
contractual terms that forbid them null and void (Article 8). Users in other
jurisdictions should review their local law and Voicemod's Terms of Use before
using this tool. Running it may still conflict with Voicemod's Terms of Use and
could put an account at risk; that decision rests with the user.

